---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Flow from '../../components/Flow.astro';
import Shot from '../../components/Shot.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';
import { darkhorseFlow } from '../../data/diagrams';
import tactics from '../../assets/projects/darkhorse/fig-tactics.jpg';
import quotes from '../../assets/projects/darkhorse/fig-quotes.jpg';
import notCalibrated from '../../assets/projects/darkhorse/fig-not-calibrated.jpg';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'darkhorse')!;
---
<ProjectLayout
  project={p}
  title="DarkHorse AI — anti-fraud verdicts that show their evidence · Tejas More"
  facts={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo — product, detection, backend, frontend, infra' },
    { label: 'For', value: 'People and small teams without a security team' },
    { label: 'Threats', value: 'Invoice fraud, CEO fraud, phishing' },
    { label: 'Inputs', value: 'Text, links, .eml files, screenshots, voice notes' },
  ]}>
  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">01 — The problem</p>
    <h2 data-split>Fraud stopped being a technology problem. It became a psychology problem.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        The expensive attacks now look exactly like ordinary work: a <strong>real</strong> supplier invoice, intercepted so
        that only the bank details changed; a message from a <strong>genuinely compromised</strong> colleague’s mailbox; a
        deadline, an instruction not to tell anyone, and a request that is individually reasonable.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of that is detectable by looking for malware. It is detectable by reading what the message is doing to the
        reader. And the whole category has a second failure: everyone claims “99% accurate” on a number nobody can check —
        while a product that cries wolf gets switched off, after which it protects nobody.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={tactics}
      alt="DarkHorse verdict, “The tactics used here”: “A deadline that does not really exist”, with why it works and what breaks it."
      caption="From a verdict: the tactic the message used, why it works, and what breaks it."
    />
    <blockquote data-reveal>
      So DarkHorse publishes a number people can hold it to — and would rather say “I could not check that” than produce a
      comfortable score.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">02 — How it works</p>
    <h2 data-split>Six independent signals. One honest verdict.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Three services. <strong>Core</strong>, the detection engine, runs in Docker on Render because it needs system binaries
        — Tesseract for screenshots, zbar for QR codes — that a serverless function cannot install. <strong>Guard</strong>,
        the web app, sits on Vercel’s CDN. The browser never talks to Core: everything goes through Guard’s same-origin
        proxy, so the session cookie stays first-party (Safari blocks third-party cookies outright), and the proxy is an
        allowlist of twelve paths rather than a blocklist.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Flow diagram={darkhorseFlow} />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">03 — The four rules</p>
    <h2 data-split>Enforced in code and tests, not in documentation.</h2>
    <div class="tiles" data-stagger>
      <div class="tile">
        <span class="label">Rule 1</span>
        <h3>Never fail open</h3>
        <p>A signal that could not run reports <span class="unavailable">UNAVAILABLE</span>, never 0. The verdict carries <code>degraded: true</code> and names what was missed.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <span class="label">Rule 2</span>
        <h3>Every non-zero score has evidence</h3>
        <p>If the model claims a finding and none of its quotations verify, the signal reports UNAVAILABLE rather than scoring it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <span class="label">Rule 3</span>
        <h3>Isolation lives in the database</h3>
        <p>Row-level security is <code>FORCE</code>d so even the table owner is subject, and the app role is <code>NOBYPASSRLS</code>. A forgotten <code>WHERE</code> cannot leak.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <span class="label">Rule 4</span>
        <h3>Context changes the words, never the number</h3>
        <p>A user’s profession changes the vocabulary of the explanation and which playbook entry is highlighted. It never changes the probability.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">04 — The six signals</p>
    <h2 data-split>Each one is independent, returns evidence, and can say “I couldn’t run.”</h2>
    <div class="tiles" data-stagger>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">auth</span><h3>Sender authentication</h3><p>SPF, DKIM and DMARC verified from raw headers. Catches a forged <code>From:</code>.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">domain</span><h3>Registration age</h3><p>How long the sending domain has existed — fresh domains are a classic tell.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">lookalike</span><h3>Impersonation</h3><p>Domains that read as the real one: ASCII homoglyphs, brand names inside other domains, and a one-character edit check, each kept only after it was measured.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">url</span><h3>Link reputation</h3><p>Every link checked and recorded as evidence, capped at 40 per message.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">attachment</span><h3>File analysis</h3><p>Type detected from content, not extension; macros in Office files; mismatches flagged.</p></div>
      <div class="tile"><span class="label">coercion · the only model call</span><h3>Persuasion analysis</h3><p>Seven levers — authority, urgency, scarcity, reciprocity, social proof, isolation, fear — multiplied by what the message asks you to do. Urgency in a message that asks nothing is just tone.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" data-reveal>
      <h3>The obvious lookalike check flagged 11.1% of legitimate domains. So it was removed.</h3>
      <pre class="formula" tabindex="0">measured on 1,607 legitimate domains (SpamAssassin ham) and on phishing feeds

                    config               legit     feeds
edit distance 2     10k brands, len 4   11.139%   1.624%   <span class="no">removed</span>
edit distance 1     1k brands, len 6     0.062%   0.076%   <span class="ok">kept</span>
brand token         500 brands, len 5    0.124%   0.567%   <span class="ok">kept</span>
ascii homoglyph     10k brands           0.000%   0.038%   <span class="ok">kept</span>

whole signal, re-measured:  3 / 1,607 = 0.19% false positives   (was 12.01%)</pre>
      <p>
        Edit distance against the top 10,000 brands was built first. It read applix.com as apple.com and amanda.org as
        panda.org, and every one of those would have been a customer asking why their supplier was quarantined. At every
        setting it fired more often on legitimate mail than on the phishing feeds, so it was deleted, and what remains was
        measured before it was kept.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">05 — The most important decision</p>
    <div class="callout" data-reveal>
      <h3>An LLM asked to find persuasion will invent it. So every quote is checked.</h3>
      <pre class="formula" tabindex="0">model returns   lever = urgency
                quote = "before the board call at 4pm"

verify_spans(quote, original_message)
  exact or whitespace-insensitive match  →  <span class="ok">evidence, with character offsets</span>
  not found                              →  <span class="no">finding discarded, not scored</span>
  every quotation fails                  →  <span class="no">UNAVAILABLE — never zero</span></pre>
      <p>
        Nothing looser than whitespace-insensitive matching is accepted, because looser starts accepting paraphrase — and
        paraphrase is where fabrication hides. The guard’s own cost was measured across 399 real messages: it discarded
        1 of 15 levers and 2 of 8 requested actions. Two of eight is a count, not a rate (the interval is roughly 7–59%),
        and the module says so.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={quotes}
      alt="DarkHorse evidence: Pressure and persuasion, CHECKED, listing three phrases quoted from the message."
      caption="What reaches the screen: only quotes found in the message itself — “before the board call at 4pm” among them."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">06 — The number it publishes</p>
    <h2 data-split>A false-positive rate anyone can reproduce.</h2>
    <div class="numbers" data-stagger>
      <div><b>1.355%</b><span>false positives — 9 of 664 legitimate messages</span></div>
      <div><b>11.1%</b><span>recall at that threshold — 6 of 54 malicious</span></div>
      <div><b>0.460</b><span>decision threshold</span></div>
      <div><b>718</b><span>messages in the frozen held-out set</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        The set is fingerprinted (<code>9fc2bac7e80a21ce</code>) and the number reproduces with one command,
        <code>python -m darkhorse.eval</code>. Its scope is stated next to it: three of six signals, network off, on corpora
        that predate 2010 — a floor on a narrow slice, not a verdict on the whole system. Recall is low because the product is
        tuned hard against false alarms, and the page says that too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">07 — The number I withdrew</p>
    <div class="callout" data-reveal>
      <h3>An earlier, better-looking figure — 0.55% — was published, then withdrawn.</h3>
      <p>
        It could not be reproduced in any configuration. Worse, the pair it was published with (0.55% false positives at
        1.96% recall) is not on the achievable curve at all: recall falls as the threshold rises, so 1.96% recall must cost
        <em>more</em> false positives than 0.925%, never fewer. The method had never been recorded next to the number, so the
        gap could be reported but not diagnosed.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong style="color:#fff">The rule since: the number and the command that produces it ship together</strong> — and
        the landing page publishes the count alongside the rate.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">08 — Calibration, honestly</p>
    <h2 data-split>The verdict tells you when its probability isn’t one.</h2>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Today the six signals combine by <strong>noisy-OR, unfitted</strong>. A fitted logistic model exists in the codebase
        and is <strong>refused at load</strong> — it failed its own sanity gate, and a model that fails validation must not
        silently become the thing producing probabilities. So every verdict carries its calibration version, and the page a
        user reads says: “this score is an unfitted combination of the individual signals and is not a calibrated
        probability.”
      </p>
    </div>
    <Shot
      image={notCalibrated}
      alt="DarkHorse verdict “Nothing suspicious found”, 0% likely an attack, stating that the score is not a calibrated probability."
      caption="The same sentence on the verdict screen — here, for a message that looks genuine."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">09 — What isn’t finished</p>
    <h2 data-split>Honest limitations.</h2>
    <ol class="defects" data-stagger>
      <li><div><strong>Recall is low — 11.1% at the published threshold.</strong><p>The product is tuned hard against false positives, which costs misses.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>The eval covers three of six signals.</strong><p>Network off, corpora that predate 2010. Next: re-run with all six signals and the network on.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>The domain signal is off in deployment.</strong><p>So most live verdicts are marked partial — and say so.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>The fusion model is uncalibrated.</strong><p>Fitting one needs a labelled set large enough to fit on — which is what the in-product feedback (“what did this turn out to be?”) is collecting.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <p class="kicker">10 — Deployment and hardening</p>
    <h2 data-split>The unglamorous parts.</h2>
    <div class="numbers" data-stagger>
      <div><b>1,246</b><span>tests, CI-gated: 1,135 engine, 111 web app</span></div>
      <div><b>~1.2 s</b><span>per text check, including persuasion analysis</span></div>
      <div><b>0.4–1.4 ms</b><span>for the deterministic signals alone</span></div>
      <div><b>$0.00048</b><span>model cost per check, measured in evaluation</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" data-reveal>
      <p>
        Row-level security verified in production, not only in tests. Argon2 password hashing, rate limiting, a hash-chained
        audit trail, GDPR-shaped account erasure, and a multi-stage Docker image with no compiler at runtime. Since 11
        October the 90-day retention sweep runs daily, and backups are taken by the project itself and restore-verified,
        with the row-level-security policy count checked afterwards, because that is what a restore silently loses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
